WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.280
Performing React Native upgrades reactively,

2
00:00:03.280 --> 00:00:07.440
waiting until a specific third-party library demands a newer version

3
00:00:07.440 --> 00:00:09.680
is a flawed engineering strategy.

4
00:00:09.680 --> 00:00:13.280
It creates invisible structural pressure across your codebase

5
00:00:13.280 --> 00:00:16.080
until dependencies eventually fracture.

6
00:00:16.080 --> 00:00:17.920
To plan a proactive roadmap,

7
00:00:17.920 --> 00:00:20.480
engineering teams must establish a baseline

8
00:00:20.480 --> 00:00:24.160
built around React Native's official service level agreement.

9
00:00:24.160 --> 00:00:28.160
That SLA dictates that only the two most recent minor versions

10
00:00:28.160 --> 00:00:31.920
receive back-ported security patches and critical bug fixes.

11
00:00:31.920 --> 00:00:34.800
This diagram charts the rolling support window

12
00:00:34.800 --> 00:00:36.880
as new stable versions release,

13
00:00:36.880 --> 00:00:38.880
official supports slides forward.

14
00:00:38.880 --> 00:00:41.360
This green area represents the safe zone,

15
00:00:41.360 --> 00:00:43.920
keeping infrastructure officially supported.

16
00:00:43.920 --> 00:00:46.960
Drop behind that window patches stop entirely.

17
00:00:46.960 --> 00:00:50.240
The core team will not issue fixes for older versions.

18
00:00:50.240 --> 00:00:53.040
This establishes the strict operational rule.

19
00:00:53.040 --> 00:00:55.920
If a project falls three or more minor versions behind

20
00:00:55.920 --> 00:00:57.440
the current stable release,

21
00:00:57.440 --> 00:01:00.800
it triggers the need for an immediate planned upgrade sprint.

22
00:01:00.800 --> 00:01:03.200
Relying on reactive patching

23
00:01:03.200 --> 00:01:08.160
leaves enterprise infrastructure exposed to unpatchable native layer flaws.

24
00:01:08.160 --> 00:01:10.880
Adhering to a proactive upgrade roadmap

25
00:01:10.880 --> 00:01:14.240
is a requirement for long-term platform survival.

26
00:01:14.240 --> 00:01:17.120
When confronting a significant version deficit,

27
00:01:17.120 --> 00:01:20.320
tech leads face a primary architectural decision,

28
00:01:20.320 --> 00:01:23.440
attempt an incremental, minor-by-miner upgrade,

29
00:01:23.520 --> 00:01:27.520
or scaffold an entirely fresh project and migrate the source code.

30
00:01:27.520 --> 00:01:29.760
This decision is dictated by the volume

31
00:01:29.760 --> 00:01:32.400
of accumulated native configuration debt.

32
00:01:32.400 --> 00:01:35.200
Every custom modification to the native build files

33
00:01:35.200 --> 00:01:38.320
adds a layer of complexity to the upgrade process.

34
00:01:38.320 --> 00:01:41.200
This flow chart maps the basic decision tree.

35
00:01:41.200 --> 00:01:43.680
For a deficit of five versions or fewer,

36
00:01:43.680 --> 00:01:46.320
the strategy is incremental execution.

37
00:01:46.320 --> 00:01:49.040
However, a hidden variable alters this,

38
00:01:49.040 --> 00:01:51.280
heavily customized native files.

39
00:01:51.280 --> 00:01:52.880
Once this count crosses 20,

40
00:01:52.880 --> 00:01:54.720
the flow chart dynamically reroutes

41
00:01:54.720 --> 00:01:56.800
to mandate a new project setup.

42
00:01:56.800 --> 00:01:58.960
Manually resolving merge conflicts

43
00:01:58.960 --> 00:02:01.120
in over 20 core native files

44
00:02:01.120 --> 00:02:03.520
typically consumes more engineering hours

45
00:02:03.520 --> 00:02:05.760
than simply scaffolding a clean project

46
00:02:05.760 --> 00:02:07.440
and porting the screens.

47
00:02:07.440 --> 00:02:09.200
At this level of technical debt,

48
00:02:09.200 --> 00:02:12.400
a fresh build is the fastest route to modernization.

49
00:02:12.400 --> 00:02:15.760
It allows you to bypass hours of configuration troubleshooting

50
00:02:15.760 --> 00:02:18.960
and favor of a clean reproducible architecture.

51
00:02:19.040 --> 00:02:21.440
For most teams, the current technical priority

52
00:02:21.440 --> 00:02:23.840
is the Tier-1 migration roadmap.

53
00:02:23.840 --> 00:02:26.800
Getting a legacy app to version 0.76

54
00:02:26.800 --> 00:02:31.200
is a complex operation that must be crossed in sequence phases.

55
00:02:31.200 --> 00:02:33.680
This pipeline illustrates the migration sequence

56
00:02:33.680 --> 00:02:37.200
beginning at 0.72 to 0.73.

57
00:02:37.200 --> 00:02:39.040
The JavaScript core is stripped out,

58
00:02:39.040 --> 00:02:41.360
making Hermes the exclusive engine.

59
00:02:41.360 --> 00:02:43.840
Notice the dependency shift below the node.

60
00:02:43.840 --> 00:02:47.760
This swap forces an update from JDK 11 to 17,

61
00:02:47.760 --> 00:02:50.960
failing to update CI build agents to JDK 17

62
00:02:50.960 --> 00:02:54.000
before merging will cause immediate pipeline failures.

63
00:02:54.000 --> 00:02:57.120
Once agents are updated and the engine is stable,

64
00:02:57.120 --> 00:03:00.800
focus shifts along the pipeline to the next upgrade phase.

65
00:03:00.800 --> 00:03:04.240
Phase two covers the jump to 0.74.

66
00:03:04.240 --> 00:03:07.360
Here, the turbo module interop layer stabilizes

67
00:03:07.360 --> 00:03:10.560
and metrobundler exports are enabled by default.

68
00:03:10.560 --> 00:03:13.600
This phase acts as a pure diagnostic step.

69
00:03:13.600 --> 00:03:15.120
Running the application here

70
00:03:15.120 --> 00:03:17.840
exposes exactly which third-party libraries

71
00:03:17.840 --> 00:03:22.000
possess underlying interop issues with the forthcoming architecture.

72
00:03:22.000 --> 00:03:24.520
You must freeze the upgrade pipeline at this stage

73
00:03:24.520 --> 00:03:27.320
to resolve those library incompatibilities.

74
00:03:27.320 --> 00:03:30.600
Fixing them now prevents unmanageable cascading failures

75
00:03:30.600 --> 00:03:33.560
from occurring during the final architectural switch.

76
00:03:33.560 --> 00:03:37.280
React native upgrades are enterprise-wide infrastructure events.

77
00:03:37.280 --> 00:03:39.440
The work is not limited to JavaScript.

78
00:03:39.440 --> 00:03:41.280
It requires a concurrent overhaul

79
00:03:41.280 --> 00:03:43.600
of the build environment and pipeline.

80
00:03:43.600 --> 00:03:48.680
Returning to our pipeline, the shift from 0.74 to 0.75

81
00:03:48.680 --> 00:03:51.080
introduces forced infrastructural updates

82
00:03:51.080 --> 00:03:53.360
impacting both operating systems.

83
00:03:53.360 --> 00:03:55.400
We evaluate Android first.

84
00:03:55.400 --> 00:03:58.520
As shown in the data block, the minimum API level

85
00:03:58.520 --> 00:04:01.160
bumps from 23 to 24.

86
00:04:01.160 --> 00:04:05.160
Dropping API 23 alters the supported device floor,

87
00:04:05.160 --> 00:04:07.800
preventing installation on older hardware,

88
00:04:07.800 --> 00:04:10.200
requiring stakeholder alignment.

89
00:04:10.200 --> 00:04:13.280
For iOS, the infrastructure mandates a baseline

90
00:04:13.280 --> 00:04:18.080
of Xcode 15.1 and the app delegate transitions to Swift.

91
00:04:18.080 --> 00:04:21.720
DevOps teams must verify and align all CI build agents

92
00:04:21.720 --> 00:04:25.040
with these precise Xcode and SDK specifications

93
00:04:25.040 --> 00:04:28.080
before the engineering team pulls the upgrade branch.

94
00:04:28.080 --> 00:04:30.400
Frontend code will simply fail to compile

95
00:04:30.400 --> 00:04:33.360
if the DevOps pipeline is not updated concurrently.

96
00:04:33.360 --> 00:04:35.560
The JavaScript layer is entirely dependent

97
00:04:35.560 --> 00:04:37.360
on this infrastructure floor.

98
00:04:37.360 --> 00:04:39.760
With the infrastructure secured, teams face

99
00:04:39.760 --> 00:04:43.360
the final and most impactful phase of the Tier 1 migration.

100
00:04:43.360 --> 00:04:47.600
The pipeline culminates here, arriving at version 0.76.

101
00:04:47.600 --> 00:04:49.280
In this version, the new architecture

102
00:04:49.280 --> 00:04:51.880
is enabled by default, moving the framework away

103
00:04:51.880 --> 00:04:55.040
from the legacy bridge toward the new JSI-based communication

104
00:04:55.040 --> 00:04:55.880
layer.

105
00:04:55.880 --> 00:05:00.160
Tech leads must allocate a dedicated isolated sprint solely

106
00:05:00.160 --> 00:05:02.760
for executing this architectural transition,

107
00:05:02.760 --> 00:05:05.240
entirely separate from feature development.

108
00:05:05.240 --> 00:05:08.280
For baseline planning, a mid-sized application typically

109
00:05:08.280 --> 00:05:10.880
requires one to two days of engineering time

110
00:05:10.880 --> 00:05:13.640
per minor version jump prior to this point.

111
00:05:13.640 --> 00:05:15.520
However, if your code based contains

112
00:05:15.520 --> 00:05:18.440
custom native modules relying on the legacy bridge,

113
00:05:18.440 --> 00:05:21.120
you must add a full week of engineering time,

114
00:05:21.120 --> 00:05:25.360
specifically to navigate the jump from 0.75 to 0.76.

115
00:05:25.360 --> 00:05:27.440
Executing this final step successfully

116
00:05:27.440 --> 00:05:29.520
clears the technical debt threshold.

117
00:05:29.520 --> 00:05:33.120
Reaching 0.76 fully transitions the enterprise application

118
00:05:33.120 --> 00:05:36.600
into the modern high-performance react native ecosystem.

119
00:05:36.600 --> 00:05:38.720
For engineering teams utilizing Expo,

120
00:05:38.720 --> 00:05:40.720
the roadmap follows a different logic.

121
00:05:40.720 --> 00:05:43.760
Upgrade schedules are bound to explicit SDK releases

122
00:05:43.760 --> 00:05:46.040
rather than raw react native versions.

123
00:05:46.040 --> 00:05:48.080
This table maps the strict friction mocking

124
00:05:48.080 --> 00:05:49.640
between the two platforms.

125
00:05:49.640 --> 00:05:54.120
You can see that Expo SDK 52 is hard-locked directly

126
00:05:54.120 --> 00:05:58.480
to react native 0.76, bringing all new architecture

127
00:05:58.480 --> 00:05:59.880
readiness with it.

128
00:05:59.880 --> 00:06:02.200
Operating within this ecosystem requires

129
00:06:02.200 --> 00:06:04.560
following one unbreakable rule.

130
00:06:04.560 --> 00:06:08.480
You must never jump more than one SDK version at a time.

131
00:06:08.480 --> 00:06:11.920
The technical reasoning is deeply embedded in the platform.

132
00:06:11.920 --> 00:06:14.880
Expo's internal libraries, like location and camera

133
00:06:14.880 --> 00:06:18.440
modules, contain inter SDK native dependencies

134
00:06:18.440 --> 00:06:21.760
that instantly break if sequential versions are bypassed.

135
00:06:21.760 --> 00:06:24.320
This creates a dual upgrade reality.

136
00:06:24.320 --> 00:06:27.080
Standard command line tools can update the JavaScript side

137
00:06:27.080 --> 00:06:29.160
seamlessly, but you must explicitly

138
00:06:29.160 --> 00:06:31.840
manage the native side by rerunning pre-builds

139
00:06:31.840 --> 00:06:34.000
or following bare workflow guides.

140
00:06:34.000 --> 00:06:36.840
Expo heavily simplifies JavaScript maintenance,

141
00:06:36.840 --> 00:06:38.800
but keeping the underlying native modules

142
00:06:38.800 --> 00:06:41.280
functional, demand strict, linear adherence

143
00:06:41.280 --> 00:06:43.120
to their SDK sequencing.

144
00:06:43.120 --> 00:06:46.120
Once a project reaches version 0.76,

145
00:06:46.120 --> 00:06:49.960
it enters a new operational reality, known as Tier Q.

146
00:06:49.960 --> 00:06:52.400
The massive breaking changes are behind you.

147
00:06:52.400 --> 00:06:55.000
With the architectural foundation now stable,

148
00:06:55.000 --> 00:06:58.360
subsequent upgrades transition into additive, low friction

149
00:06:58.360 --> 00:06:59.400
bumps.

150
00:06:59.400 --> 00:07:02.400
This calendar visualizes the target operational cadence

151
00:07:02.400 --> 00:07:05.960
for teams operating in Tier 2, a standard six-week release

152
00:07:05.960 --> 00:07:06.960
cycle.

153
00:07:06.960 --> 00:07:09.200
To integrate this into agile planning,

154
00:07:09.200 --> 00:07:11.640
an assigned upgrade sprint must trigger immediately

155
00:07:11.640 --> 00:07:14.360
following a stable react native release.

156
00:07:14.360 --> 00:07:17.720
Mandating one minor version upgrade every cycle intentionally

157
00:07:17.720 --> 00:07:20.840
keeps code diffs small and highly manageable.

158
00:07:20.840 --> 00:07:23.320
Even with minimal code diffs, DevOps teams

159
00:07:23.320 --> 00:07:26.760
must continue monitoring the CI matrix for environment shifts,

160
00:07:26.760 --> 00:07:29.000
such as the mandate to update from node 18

161
00:07:29.000 --> 00:07:31.320
to node 20 in upcoming versions.

162
00:07:31.320 --> 00:07:34.440
In Tier 2, upgrading is no longer a monumental migration

163
00:07:34.440 --> 00:07:35.120
event.

164
00:07:35.120 --> 00:07:37.960
It becomes a routine six-week operational discipline

165
00:07:37.960 --> 00:07:41.520
required to maintain a secure enterprise-grade mobile application.

